    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Two battles were fought on 14 October 1806: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> thought he was facing the main Prussian army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, but it was Davout's III Corps at Auerstedt that met it, outnumbered more than two to one.</li><li>The Prussian command was divided between several officers, had no quick courier system and lost the initiative to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> before the fighting began; both the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and Schmettau were mortally or badly wounded at Auerstedt.</li><li>Within weeks most of the Prussian army had been captured, and the defeat led reformers who had served at the battle, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Scharnhorst</a>, Gneisenau and Clausewitz, to begin remaking the Prussian state.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at first refused to believe that Davout's single corps had beaten the Prussian main body, and answered the first report with &quot;Your Marshal must be seeing double!&quot;, a reference to Davout's poor eyesight.</p></aside>
    </section>

