    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1806</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> declares war and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sends 180,000 men through the Franconian Forest towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1806</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> defeats Hohenlohe's Prussians near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1806</span><span class="rf-text">Davout's III Corps defeats the main Prussian army at Auerstedt, where the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> is mortally wounded</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1806</span><span class="rf-text">A large body of Prussian troops surrenders in the Capitulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1806</span><span class="rf-text">Davout leads his III Corps into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1806</span><span class="rf-text">Hohenlohe's force surrenders after the Battle of Prenzlau</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1806</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>'s corps is surrounded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> and surrenders the next day</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1806</span><span class="rf-text">The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> ends with Ney's capture of the fortress</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-jena-auerstedt" data-v="fd2b8a859b" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>Gudin deploys at Hassenhausen</b> Gudin's Division was stopped outside Poppel by Prussian cavalry and artillery. Davout ordered it to deploy at Hassenhausen, while Schmettau's division and Blücher's cavalry formed to attack.</li><li><b>Schmettau and Blücher push Gudin back</b> Schmettau's troops and Blücher's cavalry attacked together. They pushed Gudin's troops back to the village of Hassenhausen.</li><li><b>Both sides bring up more troops</b> Wartensleben arrived with the Duke of Brunswick, who sent infantry to the Prussian left. The rest of the French cavalry came up on Gudin's left, and Friant's Division with the 12-pound artillery moved up on his right.</li><li><b>Friant's squares advance and Hassenhausen is attacked</b> The advance of the French squares forced Blücher's cavalry back, and Blücher ordered his cavalry to attack. At that moment two of Wartensleben's regiments attacked Hassenhausen.</li><li><b>The French cavalry strike from behind the hill</b> Davout hid his chasseurs à cheval behind a hill and released them at a crucial moment, putting Blücher's cavalry to flight. Brunswick then ordered a full assault on Hassenhausen.</li><li><b>The French counter-attack</b> With Brunswick and Schmettau wounded, the Prussian command broke down. The Prince of Orange arrived and his command was split between the flanks, while Morand's Division secured Gudin's left. At 11 am Davout ordered his infantry to counter-attack.</li><li><b>The Prussian centre breaks and the King orders a withdrawal</b> Schmettau's centre was broken and forced back over the Lissbach Stream. Blücher's cavalry was blown, Wartensleben was trying to reposition his troops, and the King ordered a withdrawal.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The two battles were fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fourth-coalition">War of the Fourth Coalition</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> had begun its mobilisation almost a month before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had kept a high state of readiness since the Russian refusal to accept defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-third-coalition">War of the Third Coalition</a>. He planned to force Prussia into a decisive battle, like Austerlitz, and to pre-empt a Prussian offensive. A major part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> stood in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and he decided on an advance north-east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p><p>After the Prussian declaration of war Napoleon thrust a force of 180,000 through the Franconian Forest. The Prussian army awaited him with about 130,000 Prussians and 20,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. Its command was bloated and inefficient. Key roles were divided between several officers, and the post of chief of staff was held by three, General Phull, Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</a> and Colonel Rudolf Massenbach. The confusion caused a delay of over a month before the final order of battle was prepared, and five main plans were argued over, so that the initiative passed to the French and the Prussian plans became reactions to Napoleon's movements.</p><p>There was no courier system to carry messages quickly, and a changed order cost precious minutes. Prussian doctrines had stood unchanged for more than 50 years, while the French army had been reorganised on the corps system, which Napoleon had greatly improved by 1805.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout.jpg" alt="Louis-Nicolas Davout" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Louis-Nicolas Davout</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_nicolas_davout.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Pierre-Claude Gautherot / Tito Marzocchi de Bellucci, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the French side <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> commanded in person at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, while at Auerstedt the French force was a single corps, Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Louis-Nicolas Davout</a>'s III Corps. Napoleon's force at Jena was initially 48,000 strong, and by his carefully planned and flexible dispositions he reached local superiority with a force of 96,000 men. Less than half of them fought the Prussians, since only 40,000 were sent and actually took part. He kept the Imperial Guard under his direct command. Marshal Bernadotte's I Corps was elsewhere, ordered towards Dornburg.</p><p>The Prussian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> army was divided into three armies drawn from across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. At Jena the commander was Prince Hohenlohe-Ingelfingen, and Rüchel was to bring 15,000 men from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a>. At Auerstedt the main Prussian army of 64,000 men included King Frederick William III, the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> as overall commander, and Field Marshals von Möllendorf and von Kalckreuth. Friedrich Wilhelm von Schmettau led a division and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> the cavalry. The Prussian cavalry was better than the French and its infantry formidable. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> of 1806, the model of 1754, was called "the worst in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>" by one authority, and many soldiers in the royal army were recruited non-Prussians.</p><p>Davout's III Corps, outnumbered at Auerstedt by more than two to one, was superbly trained and disciplined. His divisions were those of Étienne Gudin, Louis Friant and Morand.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/michel-ney"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-ney.jpg" alt="Michel Ney" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/michel-ney">Michel Ney</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marechal_Ney.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">François Gérard, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> began on the morning of 14 October. The French first attacked both flanks of the Prussian line while the supporting troops took position. The French general Saint-Hilaire broke through and isolated the Prussian left flank. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-ney">Marshal Ney</a> attacked without orders. His first assault succeeded, but he was overextended and under heavy artillery fire, and a Prussian counterattack enveloped him; he formed his men into a square. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> ordered Marshal Lannes to shift from the centre to help Ney and put the Imperial Guard in the weakened centre. Ney was rescued, and the Prussian commanders did not press the French weakness. Around 1 p.m. Napoleon ordered his flanks to push hard and encircle the Prussian centre. The flank attacks succeeded and the Prussians withdrew.</p><p>At Auerstedt Gudin's division was on the move from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a> before 6:30 a.m. and was stopped outside Poppel by Prussian cavalry and artillery. Davout ordered Gudin to deploy at Hassenhausen. Schmettau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> pushed Gudin back to the village. Friant's division and the 12-pound artillery arrived at 9:30 a.m., and the French formed squares. Davout hid his chasseurs à cheval behind a hill and used them against Blücher's cavalry, three of whose regiments were routed. Shortly before 10 a.m. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> ordered a full assault on Hassenhausen, but by 10 a.m. he was carried off mortally wounded, along with the badly wounded Schmettau, and the Prussian command broke down. Davout counter-attacked at 11 a.m. By noon Schmettau's centre had been forced back over the Lissbach Stream and the King ordered a withdrawal.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher.jpg" alt="Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Field_Marshal_Prince_von_Blucher.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Dawe, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> the Prussian army lost 10,000 killed or wounded, 15,000 prisoners and 150 guns; the French lost 5,000 killed, wounded or captured. At Auerstedt Davout's corps lost 7,052 officers and men killed or wounded, against Prussian casualties of 13,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at first doubted that one corps had beaten the Prussian main body. Davout was made Duke of Auerstedt and was given the honour of entering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> first, which he did on 25 October with his exhausted III Corps. Lannes, the hero of Jena, was not so honoured.</p><p>Murat's cavalry pursued the Prussians relentlessly. A large body of troops became prisoners in the Capitulation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt">Erfurt</a> on 16 October with hardly a shot fired, and Bernadotte defeated the Duke of Württemberg's Prussian Reserve Army at Halle on 17 October. Hohenlohe surrendered on 28 October after the Battle of Prenzlau, followed by the Capitulation of Pasewalk. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>, with 21,000 men at large west of the Oder, was pursued by the French and blocked from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and his corps and Winning's was surrounded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> on 6 and 7 November. The Prussians lost 3,000 killed and wounded there, and Blücher surrendered with 9,000 other prisoners. The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> ended on 11 November.</p><p>Bernadotte's inaction at Auerstedt became controversial within a week. He and Davout later became bitter enemies, and Napoleon sent him a severely worded reprimand but took no further action.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Jena–Auerstedt&#8221; cites where it covers Aftermath:</p><ul><li>D. Plunket Barton, Bernadotte and Napoleon: 1763–1810 (John Murray, 1921)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> believed after the fighting that he had faced the main Prussian army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, and so doubted Davout's first reports. The strengths on the French side are disputed ground: at Jena 48,000 grew to 96,000 in the area with 40,000 engaged, and at Auerstedt Davout is said only to have been outnumbered more than two to one. The battle is also known through later commentators such as Nikolai Orlov, who praised Davout's choice of Mount Eckartsberga as his main effort, and Martin van Creveld, who judged Napoleon's command at Jena.</p><p>What is most disputed is Bernadotte. Davout received a courier from Berthier before dawn on 14 October saying that the Prince of Ponte Corvo might march with him, but that the Emperor hoped he would be at Dornburg, and Davout passed the order on at 4 a.m. Napoleon said on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">St Helena</a> that Bernadotte's conduct was disgraceful. Contemporary evidence shows no recriminations that night, and a later search of the orders and dispatches found no order for Bernadotte to march with Davout. Colonel Ernst Marsh Lloyd suggested that Napoleon was shifting blame for Davout having to fight alone.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst.jpg" alt="Gerhard von Scharnhorst" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Gerhard von Scharnhorst</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_Bury_-_Bildnis_des_Generals_von_Scharnhorst.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Circle of Friedrich Bury, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battles showed the need to reform a very feudal Prussian state and army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Scharnhorst</a>, Gneisenau and Clausewitz, who served in them, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> and Boyen, took part in the reforms that, together with civilian reforms of the following years, began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s transformation into a modern state. That modernised Prussia took the lead in expelling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The reduction of Prussia to a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and the revolt that restored its national honour formed a key component of German nationalism.</p><p>Martin van Creveld wrote that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> had known nothing of the main action of the day, forgotten two of his corps and had one of his principal subordinates disobey in a way that would have brought a lesser man before a firing squad, yet won what was probably the great single triumph of his career.</p><p>Napoleon named a bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> after the battle, and after his defeat the Prussian contingent of the occupation forces wished to destroy it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a> temporarily renamed it. The Paris Metro station keeps the name. Near Jena hiking trails are named after Napoleon and his marshals and a museum at Cospeda tells of the battle. It was re-enacted on 14 October 2006 with 1,800 participants, and the association Jena 1806 e. V. holds commemorative events each October.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 14 October 1806 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> defeated Hohenlohe's Prussians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> while Davout's single corps beat the main Prussian army at Auerstedt. The Prussian army was largely captured within weeks, and the defeat prompted the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerhard-von-scharnhorst">Scharnhorst</a>, Gneisenau and Clausewitz.</em></p>

